#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295135 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb31b60f-3050-5ef7-b4c3-2476ae1e52f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-146" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293849}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293849} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e0b8646-041e-54fe-904d-5fd7e7631312" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6794ccbb-8ffd-5507-a0b5-d0921c89d7f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-280387" :question/problem "If $H=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix},$ then $det(H)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-7\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-7(0-(-20))+2(-3-0) \\\\  & =-7\\cdot (20)+2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-140-6 \\\\  & =-146\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295136 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18a27456-d8f3-561b-bb79-e130d6872c6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293031}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293031} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d897447-080d-5fa1-811f-5026bf48e4a4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ee0a6b76-25b3-5557-92d9-083561656751" :question/math-academy-id "q-280390" :question/problem "If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix},$ then $det(A)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}+(-3)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-2(0-0)-3(0-0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295137 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1339ed7b-7b32-5f2e-97a1-9339503ac331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293012}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293012} :answer-field/id #uuid "93fac66a-4741-5f19-907d-88bd6bf67c55" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "602c9b3b-40e9-5371-929a-34e8b2aaccf1" :question/math-academy-id "q-280393" :question/problem "If $Q=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix},$ then $det(Q)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+5\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(1\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 3)+2(4\\cdot 2-0\\cdot (-1))+5(4\\cdot 3-1\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =3(2)+2(8)+5(12+1) \\\\  & =6+16+5(13) \\\\  & =6+16+65 \\\\  & =87\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295138 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7756ac8b-4d3e-5612-bad7-e5a160c5a43f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293420}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293420} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7577202-1833-561f-80ac-32392b1ecb99" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5aadd6ce-d715-5a22-9944-d786941130cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-280394" :question/problem "If $R=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},$ then $det(R)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+0\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(3\\cdot 2-5\\cdot (-1))+2(4\\cdot 2-5\\cdot 6)+0 \\\\  & =1(6+5)+2(8-30) \\\\  & =11+2(-22) \\\\  & =11-44 \\\\  & =-33\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295139 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "199cfd6b-b7c6-5ebd-9bb2-5a78fca81a8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "69" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293039}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293039} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca1eadfe-246c-5ca2-a02d-27c7056e56da" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4c583368-3601-5727-a2d2-942c7b9558d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-280400" :question/problem "If $W=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},$ then $det(W)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =5\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5(4\\cdot 1-6\\cdot (-3))+2((-1)(1)-6\\cdot 2)+3((-1)(-3)-4\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =5(4+18)+2(-1-12)+3(3-8) \\\\  & =5(22)+2(-13)+3(-5) \\\\  & =110-26-15 \\\\  & =69\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295140 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b6fb57b-36e2-543a-ab01-e63593d507d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293544}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293544} :answer-field/id #uuid "9633885c-c66b-52e8-84a6-2cf06bb918b2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b8c1908a-4170-5ad4-8920-81bd7dfc6050" :question/math-academy-id "q-280403" :question/problem "Given that $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & x & 4 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $det(A)=37$, find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(F) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & x & 4 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}x & 4 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+0\\begin{vmatrix}3 & x \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(x\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 5)-(3\\cdot 1-4\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =2(x-20)-(3+8) \\\\  & =2x-40-11 \\\\  & =2x-51\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $37,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-51 & =37 \\\\ 2x & =88 \\\\ x & =44.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295141 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd58d80d-e3cd-55e9-ab3a-7f344ff3bcd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293792}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293792} :answer-field/id #uuid "de3f45f5-c264-5c0e-b8b4-960fb94c3f56" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294706}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b09153b7-90f9-5996-a92d-2d17cff9a754" :question/math-academy-id "q-280404" :question/problem "Find the possible values of the constant $k$ given that the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}k+1 & 2k & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -2 & k & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is singular.\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det & =\\begin{vmatrix}k+1 & 2k & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -2 & k & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(k+1)\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ k & 1\\end{vmatrix}-2k\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(k+1)(5\\cdot 1-0\\cdot k)-2k(0\\cdot 1-0\\cdot -2)+3(0\\cdot k-5\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =(k+1)(5)-2k(0)+3(10) \\\\  & =5k+5+30 \\\\  & =5k+35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix is singular, the determinant is $0,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5k+35 & =0 \\\\ 5k & =-35 \\\\ k & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295151 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4fa7fe2-70f7-5b48-aad2-0f11a308a02b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293639}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293639} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff1893bf-705b-5320-b68c-49a5e5fad6f0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294826}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cae37ba1-c114-55f8-b121-0094f150645e" :question/math-academy-id "q-280519" :question/problem "Given that $G=\\begin{bmatrix}x & 2x-1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & x+1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ is singular, find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det & =\\begin{vmatrix}x & 2x-1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & x+1 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =x\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ x+1 & -4\\end{vmatrix}-(2x-1)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{vmatrix}+6\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & x+1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =x(3\\cdot (-4)-0\\cdot (x+1))-(2x-1)(0)+6(0\\cdot (x+1)-3\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =x(-12)+0+6(3) \\\\  & =-12x+18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix is singular, the determinant is $0,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12x+18 & =0 \\\\ -12x & =-18 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295160 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7a14d7c-c976-5346-a0ff-1a7451eb909b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293594}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293594} :answer-field/id #uuid "744d1c09-7fab-597a-b711-9626275fab38" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294339} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e75f6dd-e85a-5f64-bcc4-624a0a4629af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292998}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292998} :answer-field/id #uuid "d728a0ab-06b9-5153-aa9d-039b19b0b1ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294689}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "854d343a-6ceb-5dbc-aed9-5a3bba384840" :question/math-academy-id "q-280540" :question/problem "Given that the determinant of $\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}-2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ x & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is $32$, find all the possible values of $x$ **in ascending order**.\n\nIn ascending order, $x=$\n{{field-1}} and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Let’s use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{x}^{2}-2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ x & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2)\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}x & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+0 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2)(-2\\cdot 0-(-1)(4))-3(x\\cdot 0-(-1)(1)) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2)(4)-3(1) \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-8-3 \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $32$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-11 & =32 \\\\ 4{x}^{2} & =43 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{43}{4} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{43}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, **in ascending order**, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295161 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0455064-a7d8-59fd-ac77-33abf41ca336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293865}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293865} :answer-field/id #uuid "718e1993-6243-5b67-ad85-b48d5e4c1b0e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294333} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5474c54-161f-5c59-93d2-847eb2a1ab3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293891}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293891} :answer-field/id #uuid "904e7486-ae2f-53e2-a8b8-33a2fef9eb06" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f11851f4-08f0-546e-8ec7-83f1d8f0d049" :question/math-academy-id "q-280541" :question/problem "Given that the determinant of $\\begin{bmatrix}x & 2x & 5 \\\\ 3 & x & 4 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ is $40$, find all the possible values of $x$ **in ascending order**.\n\nIn ascending order, $x=$\n{{field-1}} and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Let’s use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}x & 2x & 5 \\\\ 3 & x & 4 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =x\\begin{vmatrix}x & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-2x\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+5\\begin{vmatrix}3 & x \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =x(2x-4)-2x(3\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 7)+5(3-7x) \\\\  & =x(2x-4)-2x(-22)+15-35x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-4x+44x+15-35x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+5x+15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $40$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+5x+15 & =40 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+5x-25 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{-5\\pm \\sqrt{{5}^{2}-4(2)(-25)}}{2(2)} \\\\ x & =\\frac{-5+\\sqrt{225}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5\\pm 15}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, **in ascending order**, the solutions are $x=-5$ and $x=\\frac{5}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297464 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e27d4c6-c946-503d-ac45-3010dc21b71f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296023}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296023} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c1cb17d-1226-50b5-8a55-fa9762362774" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e9963779-43cf-5b65-8821-6ff0b678d8cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-316345" :question/problem "Calculate the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $a=⟨-3,5,0⟩$ and $b=⟨4,-1,2⟩.$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(5\\cdot 2-0\\cdot (-1))i-((-3)\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 4)j+((-3)\\cdot (-1)-5\\cdot 4)k \\\\  & =10i+6j-17k \\\\  & =⟨10,6,-17⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,a\\times b\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{10}^{2}+{6}^{2}+(-17)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100+36+289} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{425} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297465 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed164a80-8e70-5398-881b-db2dc37213b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296523}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296523} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa7793f2-af09-5083-a758-5dedb4543d5e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297147}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5b9773af-f53a-5ffd-bd89-0fdc3a8398e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-316350" :question/problem "Calculate the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $a=⟨1,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨0,3,4⟩.$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 4-0\\cdot 3)i-(1\\cdot 4-0\\cdot 0)j+(1\\cdot 3-0\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =0i-4j+3k \\\\  & =⟨0,-4,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,a\\times b\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+16+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297466 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62f41066-5228-5f44-a4bc-f9a7a9de4308" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295832}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295832} :answer-field/id #uuid "809afc0c-e441-5baf-a7b0-01f0a22f4f33" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296898}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ed3e1530-1ab9-5085-9128-11a28820fcba" :question/math-academy-id "q-316355" :question/problem "Calculate the area ${A}_{△}$ of the triangle spanned by $p=⟨0,5,-1⟩$ and $q=⟨6,0,1⟩.$\n\n${A}_{△}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\times q & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(5\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 0)i-(0\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 6)j+(0\\cdot 0-5\\cdot 6)k \\\\  & =5i-6j-30k \\\\  & =⟨5,-6,-30⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,p\\times q\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(-6)^{2}+(-30)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+36+900} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{961} \\\\  & =31.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{31}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297467 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77b0cc91-ce5c-5046-8f7d-a7344d76cd0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{42}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295920}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295920} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ff4772e-9511-5679-8988-c1e0ad78f24a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296670}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2e86dffd-3cdd-51b5-8002-a730ad7bd647" :question/math-academy-id "q-316361" :question/problem "Calculate the area ${A}_{△}$ of the $△\\,PQR$, if $P(1,-2,0)$, $Q(3,1,-1)$, $R(-1,0,2).$\n\n${A}_{△}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =(3i+j-k)-(i-2j) \\\\  & =2i+3j-k \\\\  & =⟨2,3,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =(-i+2k)-(i-2j) \\\\  & =-2i+2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 2)i-(2\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-2))j+(2\\cdot 2-3\\cdot (-2))k \\\\  & =8i-2j+10k \\\\  & =⟨8,-2,10⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{10}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64+4+100} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{168} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{42}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,PQR$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{42}}{2}=\\sqrt{42}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297468 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dba9f272-6930-502c-8925-212ed902a7be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{237}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296427}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296427} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d733894-4032-579e-a650-81c38b682309" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a38f6d6d-8117-5b90-a022-c6e9c7160729" :question/math-academy-id "q-316362" :question/problem "Calculate the area ${A}_{△}$ of the $△\\,DEF$, if $D(-2,1,3)$, $E(0,-1,0)$, $F(2,2,-1).$\n\n${A}_{△}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =e-d \\\\  & =(-j)-(-2i+j+3k) \\\\  & =2i-2j-3k \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =f-d \\\\  & =(2i+2j-k)-(-2i+j+3k) \\\\  & =4i+j-4k \\\\  & =⟨4,1,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE}\\times \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -4\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot (-4)-(-3)\\cdot 1)i-(2\\cdot (-4)-(-3)\\cdot 4)j+(2\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 4)k \\\\  & =11i-4j+10k \\\\  & =⟨11,-4,10⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{DE}\\times \\overset{\\to }{DF}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{11}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{10}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{121+16+100} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{237}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,DEF$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{237}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297469 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38ce5de2-b8be-5dc3-beaf-15c0bca7c3b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295588}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295588} :answer-field/id #uuid "940eef9e-f927-5a3a-861d-74d1ef0554a6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "66634719-8e4b-59db-b970-ace87e066b21" :question/math-academy-id "q-316363" :question/problem "Calculate the area ${A}_{△}$ of the $△\\,LMN$, if $L(-3,0,1)$, $M(0,2,-2)$, $N(2,-1,0).$\n\n${A}_{△}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =m-l \\\\  & =(2j-2k)-(-3i+k) \\\\  & =3i+2j-3k \\\\  & =⟨3,2,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =n-l \\\\  & =(2i-j)-(-3i+k) \\\\  & =5i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨5,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot (-1)-(-3)\\cdot (-1))i-(3\\cdot (-1)-(-3)\\cdot 5)j+(3\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 5)k \\\\  & =-5i-12j-13k \\\\  & =⟨-5,-12,-13⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+(-12)^{2}+(-13)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+144+169} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{338} \\\\  & =13\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,LMN$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{13\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297473 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c05366e-122f-5432-9545-0905387ebf8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3230}}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295700}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295700} :answer-field/id #uuid "8653ad62-f824-5e0d-9daf-40a42762bc5c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "cc3dd100-4787-576c-a303-83ee06838d2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-316392" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $NP$ of the $△\\,LMN$, if $L(3,-1,0)$, $M(0,2,1)$, $N(-2,1,-1).$\n\n$NP=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $NP$ can be related to the area of $△\\,LMN$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,NP\\cdot LM.\n$$\n\nSo, to find $NP,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =m-l \\\\  & =(2j+k)-(3i-j) \\\\  & =-3i+3j+k \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =n-l \\\\  & =(-2i+j-k)-(3i-j) \\\\  & =-5i+2j-k \\\\  & =⟨-5,2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -5 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 2)i-((-3)\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot (-5))j+((-3)\\cdot 2-3\\cdot (-5))k \\\\  & =-5i-8j+9k \\\\  & =⟨-5,-8,9⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+(-8)^{2}+{9}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{170}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,LMN$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{170}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $LM$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LM & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,NP\\cdot LM,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,NP\\cdot LM \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{170}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,NP\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ \\sqrt{170} & =NP\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ NP & =\\frac{\\sqrt{170}}{\\sqrt{19}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3230}}{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297474 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "285859fd-02f8-5227-baf4-1ae234928772" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{4186}}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295490}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295490} :answer-field/id #uuid "96ea95b7-7464-5f8e-b5f7-4c89f5a83900" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "118c9278-fa2c-5ecd-aadf-b52c033ba66c" :question/math-academy-id "q-316395" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $UT$ of the $△\\,UVW$, if $U(-2,2,0)$, $V(1,-1,2)$, $W(3,0,-1).$\n\n$UT=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $UT$ can be related to the area of $△\\,UVW$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,UT\\cdot VW.\n$$\n\nSo, to find $UT,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{VW}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{VU}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{VW} & =w-v \\\\  & =(3i-k)-(i-j+2k) \\\\  & =2i+j-3k \\\\  & =⟨2,1,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{VU} & =u-v \\\\  & =(-2i+2j)-(i-j+2k) \\\\  & =-3i+3j-2k \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{VW}\\times \\overset{\\to }{VU} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot (-2)-(-3)\\cdot 3)i-(2\\cdot (-2)-(-3)\\cdot (-3))j+(2\\cdot 3-1\\cdot (-3))k \\\\  & =7i+13j+9k \\\\  & =⟨7,13,9⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{VW}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{VU}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{VW}\\times \\overset{\\to }{VU}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{7}^{2}+{13}^{2}+{9}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{299}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,UVW$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{299}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $VW$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}VW & =|\\overset{\\to }{VW}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,UT\\cdot VW,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,UT\\cdot VW \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{299}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,UT\\cdot \\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\sqrt{299} & =UT\\cdot \\sqrt{14} \\\\ UT & =\\frac{\\sqrt{299}}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{4186}}{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297475 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1348e485-5031-58d0-9132-dc7a8e060aa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295371}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295371} :answer-field/id #uuid "38a7753b-1420-53a5-8abd-6a0b570e42f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "041de7f5-8dc3-5943-9410-cd670ed64e0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-316400" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $VL$ of the $△\\,TUV$, if $T(1,0,1)$, $U(3,2,-1)$, $V(-1,1,0).$\n\n$VL=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $VL$ can be related to the area of $△\\,TUV$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}VL\\cdot TU\n$$\n\nSo, to find $VL,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{TU}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{TV}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{TU} & =u-t \\\\  & =(3i+2j-k)-(i+k) \\\\  & =2i+2j-2k \\\\  & =⟨2,2,-2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{TV} & =v-t \\\\  & =(-i+j)-(i+k) \\\\  & =-2i+j-k \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{TU}\\times \\overset{\\to }{TV} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 1)i-(2\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot (-2))j+(2\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-2))k \\\\  & =0i+6j+6k \\\\  & =⟨0,6,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{TU}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{TV}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{TU}\\times \\overset{\\to }{TV}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{6}^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{72} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,TUV$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{2}=3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $TU$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TU & =|\\overset{\\to }{TU}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}VL\\cdot TU,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}VL\\cdot TU \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}VL\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & =VL\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ VL & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ VL & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297477 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff61a221-719e-515d-a51f-fa366df765f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{38}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296631}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296631} :answer-field/id #uuid "49802bb4-f9df-54ac-8e86-92d7c96102d2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0e0cc38a-3052-5bd2-aef9-6d95ea796618" :question/math-academy-id "q-316409" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $IP$ of the $△\\,HIJ$, if $H(-1,2,0)$, $I(1,1,3)$, $J(0,-2,1).$\n\n$IP=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $IP$ can be related to the area of $△\\,HIJ$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}IP\\cdot HJ\n$$\n\nSo, to find $IP,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{HJ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{HI}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{HJ} & =j-h \\\\  & =(-2j+k)-(-i+2j) \\\\  & =i-4j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,-4,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{HI} & =i-h \\\\  & =(i+j+3k)-(-i+2j) \\\\  & =2i-j+3k \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{HJ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{HI} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-4)\\cdot 3-1\\cdot (-1))i-(1\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 2)j+(1\\cdot (-1)-(-4)\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =-11i-j+7k \\\\  & =⟨-11,-1,7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{HJ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{HI}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{HJ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{HI}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-11)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{7}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{171} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,HIJ$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{19}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $HJ$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}HJ & =|\\overset{\\to }{HJ}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}IP\\cdot HJ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}IP\\cdot HJ \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{19}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,IP\\cdot 3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{19} & =\\sqrt{2}\\,IP \\\\ IP & =\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{38}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297486 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "970f719c-7b09-55f2-929e-43449f0a9042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296074}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296074} :answer-field/id #uuid "97996f38-e573-5502-9f72-9a2d0b28eaf9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1c80aa6a-7a46-5942-80d2-93913627438b" :question/math-academy-id "q-316514" :question/problem "Calculate the area ${A}_{△}$ of the $△\\,DEF$, if $D(-1,2,0)$, $E(2,0,1)$, $F(1,3,2).$\n\n${A}_{△}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =e-d \\\\  & =(2i+k)-(-i+2j) \\\\  & =3i-2j+k \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =f-d \\\\  & =(i+3j+2k)-(-i+2j) \\\\  & =2i+j+2k \\\\  & =⟨2,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE}\\times \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1)i-(3\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 1)j+(3\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =-5i-4j+7k \\\\  & =⟨-5,-4,7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{DE}\\times \\overset{\\to }{DF}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{7}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{90} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,DEF$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297487 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fe7bd94-f1f1-5968-8699-68e5eff1beb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{146}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295726}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295726} :answer-field/id #uuid "f58dd836-40be-566d-a116-79d128a9ed1d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "af1c1af8-7ccd-5f01-9374-729457ea6109" :question/math-academy-id "q-316516" :question/problem "Calculate the area ${A}_{△}$ of the $△\\,LMN$, if $L(1,0,-1)$, $M(3,2,0)$, $N(0,1,3).$\n\n${A}_{△}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =m-l \\\\  & =(3i+2j)-(i-k) \\\\  & =2i+2j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,2,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =n-l \\\\  & =(j+3k)-(i-k) \\\\  & =-i+j+4k \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 4-1\\cdot 1)i-(2\\cdot 4-(-1)\\cdot 1)j+(2\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =7i-9j+4k \\\\  & =⟨7,-9,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{7}^{2}+(-9)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{146}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,LMN$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{146}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297489 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e247391e-4dbd-55f5-93b1-53a444f611d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296472}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296472} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c517877-5eaa-568b-a6c7-60cea011507e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cf87a92d-df17-5548-a069-1e61f18f426a" :question/math-academy-id "q-316530" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,STU,$ if $S(0,1,2),$ $T(2,3,1),$ $U(4,1,2).$\n\n${A}_{△}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{ST}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{SU}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{ST} & =t-s \\\\  & =(2i+3j+k)-(j+2k) \\\\  & =2i+2j-k \\\\  & =⟨2,2,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{SU} & =u-s \\\\  & =(4i+j+2k)-(j+2k) \\\\  & =4i+0j+0k \\\\  & =⟨4,0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{ST}\\times \\overset{\\to }{SU} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 0)i-(2\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 4)j+(2\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 4)k \\\\  & =0i-4j-8k \\\\  & =⟨0,-4,-8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{ST}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{SU}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{ST}\\times \\overset{\\to }{SU}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{80} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,STU$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{2}=2\\sqrt{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297491 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a47b6ed-480c-5e70-aefa-cd3ffcab87ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{806}}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295507}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295507} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd373048-f3c1-5400-8d49-9bc5ec200162" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3aadd472-6c53-5587-bcdd-18195d95d688" :question/math-academy-id "q-316553" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CK$ of the $△\\,ABC,$ if $A(1,0,1),$ $B(4,2,1),$ $C(2,3,2).$\n\n$CK=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CK$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CK\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CK,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(4i+2j+k)-(i+k) \\\\  & =3i+2j+0k \\\\  & =⟨3,2,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(2i+3j+2k)-(i+k) \\\\  & =i+3j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,3,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 3)i-(3\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 1)j+(3\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =2i-3j+7k \\\\  & =⟨2,-3,7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{7}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{62}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{62}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CK\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{62}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CK\\cdot \\sqrt{13} \\\\ CK & =\\frac{\\sqrt{62}}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ CK & =\\frac{\\sqrt{806}}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297492 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7fbf2db-fc7b-5658-bc9b-3b51bd33b591" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{110}}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296318}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296318} :answer-field/id #uuid "659b57b6-4355-5ee2-9530-0067f725247f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9fd89971-85c9-5581-b57b-a897961ff335" :question/math-academy-id "q-316556" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $HK$ of the $△\\,GHI,$ if $G(1,1,1),$ $H(3,2,4),$ $I(2,4,2).$\n\n$HK=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $HK$ can be related to the area of $△\\,GHI$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}HK\\cdot GI\n$$\n\nSo, to find $HK,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{GI}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{GH}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{GI} & =i-g \\\\  & =(2i+4j+2k)-(i+j+k) \\\\  & =i+3j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,3,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{GH} & =h-g \\\\  & =(3i+2j+4k)-(i+j+k) \\\\  & =2i+j+3k \\\\  & =⟨2,1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{GI}\\times \\overset{\\to }{GH} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 1)i-(1\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 2)j+(1\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =8i-j-5k \\\\  & =⟨8,-1,-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{GI}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{GH}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{GI}\\times \\overset{\\to }{GH}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,GHI$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $GI$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}GI & =|\\overset{\\to }{GI}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}HK\\cdot GI,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}HK\\cdot \\sqrt{11} \\\\ HK & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\ HK & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{110}}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297493 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8a61533-070f-501b-8382-171a4281490f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{22}}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296320}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296320} :answer-field/id #uuid "51a19f72-85aa-5439-92e2-7ba79108e904" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "05e91010-8b10-50cc-b3a2-21f43d310a7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-316558" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $TK$ of the $△\\,RST,$ if $R(4,5,6),$ $S(7,4,5),$ $T(6,8,4).$\n\n$TK=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $TK$ can be related to the area of $△\\,RST$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}TK\\cdot RS\n$$\n\nSo, to find $TK,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{RS}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{RT}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{RS} & =s-r \\\\  & =(7i+4j+5k)-(4i+5j+6k) \\\\  & =3i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨3,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{RT} & =t-r \\\\  & =(6i+8j+4k)-(4i+5j+6k) \\\\  & =2i+3j-2k \\\\  & =⟨2,3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{RS}\\times \\overset{\\to }{RT} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)(-2)-(-1)\\cdot 3)i-(3(-2)-(-1)\\cdot 2)j+(3\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =5i+4j+11k \\\\  & =⟨5,4,11⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{RS}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{RT}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{RS}\\times \\overset{\\to }{RT}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{11}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{162} \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,RST$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $RS$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RS & =|\\overset{\\to }{RS}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}TK\\cdot RS,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}TK\\cdot \\sqrt{11} \\\\ TK & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\ TK & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{22}}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299657 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "425e3407-b831-5dfb-8a4e-ab70c457a4ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297820}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297820} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcec9a87-0fd2-5ac6-945b-1c415bc62f4f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "18bcf01e-256b-5339-97ad-00f972b0c662" :question/math-academy-id "q-317910" :question/problem "If $a=⟨5,1⟩$ and $b=⟨2,4⟩,$ then $a\\cdot b=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =10+4 \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299658 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35fdb316-399f-5821-b5d4-b5ce24c7099e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297769}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297769} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dca9ebc-819e-5579-86a0-5f4b732b025c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "02b21351-2b17-5c15-a1fb-09ee1d2960d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-317911" :question/problem "If $u=⟨8,-2⟩$ and $v=⟨3,6⟩,$ then $u\\cdot v=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{x}\\cdot {v}_{x}+{u}_{y}\\cdot {v}_{y} \\\\  & =8\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =24-12 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299660 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "050de492-c23d-5e5a-9515-f5292836bf29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297577}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297577} :answer-field/id #uuid "af74f5ad-0374-5f9e-bd30-ae2ff4e64785" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6840a760-5144-5da8-bd56-8faedecfd9c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-317932" :question/problem "If $c=\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ 2k \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},$ $d=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},$ and $c⊥d,$ then $k=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We know that $c⊥d$ ($c$ and $d$ are perpendicular) if and only if $c\\cdot d=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot d & ={c}_{x}\\cdot {d}_{x}+{c}_{y}\\cdot {d}_{y}+{c}_{z}\\cdot {d}_{z} \\\\  & =k\\cdot (-3)+2k\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-3k+4k+8 \\\\  & =k+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot d & =0 \\\\ k+8 & =0 \\\\ k & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299664 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc42bda9-d3c0-5507-b933-0b2f39398215" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298373}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298373} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0941247-a978-51c6-9a76-00d8d070c636" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d22ee02b-54e6-511a-8cc5-59280c888981" :question/math-academy-id "q-317945" :question/problem "Let $a=⟨2{x}^{2},-4⟩$ and $b=⟨8,4⟩.$ If $a⊥b$ and $x>0,$ then $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We know that $a⊥b$ ($a$ and $b$ are perpendicular) if and only if $a\\cdot b=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}\\cdot 8+(-4)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =16{x}^{2}-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ 16{x}^{2}-16 & =0 \\\\ 16{x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are told that $x>0.$ Therefore, $x=1.$"}]}
